<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trachonitis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lajat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trachonitis was a rugged lava field south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, now known as the Lajat. Its caves sheltered brigands, and Augustus gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> in the 20s BCE to suppress them. It later formed part of the tetrarchy of Philip and of the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Agrippa I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrippa-ii">Agrippa II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trachonitis.jpg" alt="Trachonitis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anas Qtiesh · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lajat,_Syria2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Lajat is the largest lava field in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, covering some 900 square kilometres about 50 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Hauran</a> plain to the west and the foothills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a> to the south. It lies 600 to 700 metres above sea level, and its highest volcanic cone, just west of Shahba, reaches 1,159 metres. Jagged basalt boulders cover most of it, with patches of arable land in shallow depressions. Springs are scarce, and its villages store rainwater in cisterns. The Greek name means the land of the rugged stony tract; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> calls the region Argob.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For much of the 1st and 2nd millennia BCE the region was influenced by the Arameans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>, and in the 2nd century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> annexed it. To deal with the brigands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a> settled 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumaeans</a> there, and around 7 BCE he invited Zamaris, a Jew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, with 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> to settle Bathyra in Batanea. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> the region passed to the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built a road through its centre, and towns such as Shahba and Zorava, today Izra', grew up between the 1st and 4th centuries.</p><p>Under Byzantine rule building flourished, and Zorava, where the martyrium of Saint George replaced a pagan temple in 515, became a bishopric in 542; by the mid-6th century most of its Arab inhabitants were Christians. The region was later abandoned, then resettled by refugees during the Mongol invasions of the 13th century, which gave it the Arabic name al-Laja, the refuge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> migrants from Wadi al-Taym and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> settled the Lajat from the late 18th century, above all after the 1860 Mount Lebanon civil war, and clashed with the Bedouin Sulut in 1868. Druze live in its central and eastern parts, and Muslims and Melkite Christians in villages along its western edge. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> made the Lajat a World Biosphere Reserve in 2009.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lajat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Philip received the lands north and east of the Sea of Galilee: Gaulanitis, Batanaea, <b>Trachonitis</b>, Auranitis and part of Ituraea, a frontier zone the Romans had entrusted to Herod to suppress brigandage.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-philip">Tetrarchy of Philip</a></li></ul></section>
